    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d124">

                <pb facs="0522" n="402" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg402"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.51 W 89 France/10</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The French Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Jusserand</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Translation<anchor n="23" xml:id="d124-f1"
                        />]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-06-30"><hi rendition="#italic">June 30,
                        1922</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Secretary of State</hi>: With special reference
                    to Your Excellency’s note of April 22, last,<anchor n="24" xml:id="d124-f2"/> I
                    have, by order of my Government, the honor to inform you that Mr. Parmentier,
                    Director of the General Handling of Funds of the Ministry of Finance, has been
                    commissioned to enter into relations with the American Commission on War Foreign
                    Debts. He will shortly sail for the United States on board <hi
                        rendition="#italic">La Savoie.</hi></p>
                <p>He will be accompanied by Mr. Le Neveu, Secretary of Embassy, assigned for duty
                    at the Ministry of Commerce.</p>
                <p>Be pleased [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Jusserand</hi></signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>P. S. I should be very thankful to the Department of State if you would
                        kindly direct that the necessary steps be taken to facilitate the landing of
                        these two French citizens sent on a mission to the United States. J.</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
